ai-digest Repository Version History
====================================

v1.2.1 (2026-08-09)
-------------------
- Add .gitattributes, marking the Markdown documents for diff.
- Rename doc/POLICY and doc/LICENSE to doc/POLICY.md and doc/LICENSE.md, so
  that GitHub renders them, and update the references to them.
- Expand doc/POLICY.md into a standalone policy covering environment
  differences, repeated execution, naming, the scope and history of a pull
  request, and the width a version history entry keeps.
- Summarize the entries of this file to one bullet per change, as
  doc/POLICY.md has always asked.
- Add the shared version history description guidelines to the end of this
  file.

v1.2 (2026-08-05)
-----------------
- Rename the endpoint settings from ANTHROPIC_* and OPENAI_* to a single
  SUMMARIZER_* set, and name the wire protocol in SUMMARIZER_BACKEND as
  anthropic-compatible or openai-compatible; the command line options
  follow the variables they override.
- Refuse the superseded setting names at startup. Upgrading a host means
  unsetting them wherever they were exported, not only editing .env.
- Bound the summarization request with SUMMARIZER_TIMEOUT, defaulting to
  180 seconds, so that HTTP_TIMEOUT now covers only the collectors and the
  scraper.
- Raise the MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS default to 8000 and the HTTP_TIMEOUT default
  to 60 seconds, and move the viewer from port 5000 to 3000. A deployment
  relying on the defaults answers on the new port after an upgrade.
- Document USER_AGENT in .env.example.
- Add doc/DEPLOYMENT.md and deploy/, covering the Debian and nginx
  procedure and the routine operations.
- Describe the exit codes, the failure log lines and how to add a collector
  in the README.
- List doc/POLICY and doc/VERSIONS in the repository structure and link
  them from the top of the README.

v1.1.1 (2026-08-04)
-------------------
- Read the feed timestamps as UTC, which is what feedparser returns; a host
  east of UTC had been losing the offset from its look back window.
- Name the configured look back window in the prompt and in the summary
  image instead of always announcing 24 hours.
- Report why a run collected nothing, distinguishing unreachable sources,
  sources with nothing published inside the window, and no source
  configured; a run that loses only some of its sources continues and says
  so.
- Copy the composite image of the captured report into doc/screenshots from
  tools/capture_screens.py.
- Accept every setting as a command line option on cli.py, each named after
  the variable it replaces. The three credentials deliberately have none,
  because a command line is readable by every user of the host.
- Keep the categories of the leading topics in the legend of the summary
  image.
- Draw a card instead of ending the run when an image is refused as a
  decompression bomb.
- Record the look back window a run collected in the statistics of its
  report, so that 'cli.py render' redraws the summary image with the period
  the report was built from.
- Fall back on the default identity when USER_AGENT is set but empty.
- Apply the line limit of wrap_text() to an explicit line break as well.

v1.1 (2026-08-02)
-----------------
- Stop the batch on an unknown SUMMARIZER_BACKEND value instead of falling
  back on the default, and remove the unused Config.require_api_key().
- Accept only http and https links from the collectors, and neutralize the
  ones already stored when a report is rendered.
- Enforce the image size limit while the response is being read rather than
  after the whole body has been buffered, and request image sources over
  http and https only.
- Anchor the report date pattern with \A and \Z.
- Cover the configuration, the report paths, the link filtering, the capped
  download and the plain backend with unit tests.
- Add ANTHROPIC_THINKING_MODE and ANTHROPIC_TOOL_CHOICE_MODE, the latter
  taking 'any' as well as a named tool, for an endpoint that otherwise
  never returns a tool_use block. The defaults leave the request unchanged.
- Add ANTHROPIC_TEXT_JSON_FALLBACK, off by default, which reads a report an
  endpoint wrote as JSON text rather than as a tool call.
- Read MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS from the environment, where it had been a constant
  in the summarizer.
- Add ANTHROPIC_MAX_RETRIES.
- Add SUMMARIZER_BACKEND=openai, an OpenAI-compatible Chat Completions path
  validated through the same to_topics(); the openai package stays out of
  requirements.txt and is imported only when that backend runs.
- Name the stop reason and the content block types when the summarizer
  finds no build_report call, report truncated or unparsable arguments as
  such, accept only a block named build_report, and log one summary line
  per response.
- Add doc/POLICY, the ai-digest implementation policy adapted for a single
  Python project from the shared id774/scripts policy.

v1.0 (2026-07-31)
-----------------
- Add the 'demo' command, which builds a report from the sample bundled in
  ai_digest/demo without collecting anything or calling the API, and
  document the output with screenshots captured from it by
  tools/capture_screens.py.
- Add SUMMARIZER_BACKEND=plain, letting the batch pipeline run without an
  Anthropic API key by building topics mechanically instead of calling the
  Claude API.
- Support custom Anthropic-compatible API URLs and Bearer authentication.

v0.1 (2026-07-26)
-----------------
- The initial version of the project.

Version History Guidelines
==========================
- Write one coherent change per physical line, as a rule.
- Keep entries near 100 columns where practical. Necessary file names,
  command names, option names, and configuration names may extend an entry
  to approximately 120 columns.
- When a version history already exists, balance a new entry against the
  length of the existing lines, so that the whole file stays consistent.
  Holding to the form this file has settled on comes before the one physical
  line above, and leaves that rule standing wherever no such form exists.
- When an entry becomes substantially longer, omit or abstract implementation
  details, examples, reasons, and secondary effects where possible.
- Preserve the changed target, externally observable behavior, compatibility
  or security impact, and important option or configuration names.
- Combine related changes within the same version, especially changes to the
  same file or feature.
- Place closely related entries near each other.
- Append an independent new change to the end of the current version entry.
